# Review routing. Deliberately NOT a global owner — most of the tree reviews
# fine on its own. These paths decide what ships, how it is built, or what runs
# on a stranger's machine, so they should never land without a maintainer
# looking, however small the diff:
#
#   .github/workflows/    CI and release. A workflow edit is the supply-chain
#                         surface: it can publish assets, and on a fork PR it
#                         runs attacker-authored steps (read-only token, but
#                         still execution).
#   install.sh            what `curl … | sh` executes, as root-adjacent as this
#                         project gets.
#   scripts/              the single writer of the version across four
#                         manifests, and the bundle-freshness gate.
#   electron/             the unsigned installers behind the front-page buttons,
#                         whose version-less filenames those buttons depend on.
#   backend/web/static/   the committed bundle that ships inside the wheel.
#
# GitHub auto-requests review from the owner listed here. It only *blocks* a
# merge if "Require review from Code Owners" is enabled on the branch ruleset —
# worth turning on when there is more than one committer.

/.github/workflows/   @emandel2630
/.github/CODEOWNERS   @emandel2630
/install.sh           @emandel2630
/scripts/             @emandel2630
/electron/            @emandel2630
/backend/web/static/  @emandel2630
/pyproject.toml       @emandel2630
/CLA.md               @emandel2630
/SECURITY.md          @emandel2630
